Here's the complete working MCP HTTP server:

**`Program.cs`** — Sets up HTTP transport with `MapMcp`:

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithHttpTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly()
    .WithResourcesFromAssembly();

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapMcp();

app.Run();
```

**`Tools/EchoTool.cs`** — Example tools:

```csharp
[McpServerToolType]
public static class EchoTool
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Echoes the input message back to the caller.")]
    public static string Echo([Description("The message to echo")] string message)
        => $"Echo: {message}";

    [McpServerTool, Description("Returns the current server time.")]
    public static string GetServerTime()
        => DateTime.UtcNow.ToString("O");
}
```

**`Resources/ConfigResource.cs`** — Example resources:

```csharp
[McpServerResourceType]
public static class ConfigResource
{
    [McpServerResource(UriTemplate = "config://app/settings", Name = "App Configuration", MimeType = "application/json")]
    [Description("Returns the application configuration data.")]
    public static string GetAppSettings() { ... }

    [McpServerResource(UriTemplate = "config://app/endpoints", Name = "API Endpoints", MimeType = "application/json")]
    [Description("Returns available API endpoint information.")]
    public static string GetEndpoints() { ... }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `WithHttpTransport()` + `app.MapMcp()` exposes the MCP endpoint at `/mcp` (Streamable HTTP)
- `WithToolsFromAssembly()` discovers `[McpServerToolType]` classes
- `WithResourcesFromAssembly()` discovers `[McpServerResourceType]` classes
- Resources use `UriTemplate` (not `Uri`) and `[Description]` as a separate attribute
- NuGet packages: `ModelContextProtocol` + `ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore` (v2.0.0-preview.1)

The project is at `/tmp/sv-79a490d975194ca5913eec9236a788a7/McpHttpServer` and builds cleanly.
